WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.899
Over the past decade, perky Progressive Insurance
saleswoman Flo has become a television mainstay,

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:07.280
appearing in dozens of commercials.

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:11.800
But while everyone knows Flo, chances are
you've never even heard of Stephanie Courtney,

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:12.800
the woman who plays the character.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:17.480
Let's change that with a look at some things
you didn't know about the woman behind Flo.

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:18.980
Mommy dearest

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:25.300
In a 2016 interview with Lohud, Courtney gave
the inside scoop on how she landed the once-in-a-lifetime

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:26.060
role of Flo.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:30.500
Turns out she had a secret weapon when she
went to the audition: her mom, Jane Courtney.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:35.000
"What they were looking for was basically
a friendly neighborhood waitress; she is super

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:38.760
friendly and nice, almost to the point of
madness, and I was like, 'I can do that.'

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.100
I went straight to my mom and I credit her
with Flo's personality.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:44.540
I said, 'Yes, I can become Jane Courtney!'"

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:45.940
Improv

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:51.160
In her original audition, Courtney had just
one line, but during her second audition,

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:52.980
the director invited her to play with the
role.

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:57.080
As a member of the famous Groundlings improv
comedy group, that was all the opening Courtney

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:57.580
needed.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:02.480
Progressive Chief Marketing Officer Jeff Carney
told Advertising Age that "Flo was an accident."

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:06.990
The character was just supposed to be a friendly
cashier in the superstore, but Courtney brought

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:11.340
the character to life when she ad-libbed the
line, "wow, I say it louder."

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:12.340
“Wow.”

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:13.120
“Wow!

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:14.080
I know!

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:14.960
I say it louder.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:15.540
Have a great day!”

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:20.260
Company execs realized the concept gave them
the chance to "surround Flo with characters

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:23.580
to play off and offered other opportunities
to expand Flo's story."

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:28.000
Since that fateful audition, Courtney has
continued to be an integral voice in how the

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.600
character of Progressive Flo has… progressed.

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:32.920
And all thanks to one little "wow."

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:35.600
It just shows what can happen if you say the
right word.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:36.500
"Slovakia.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:37.680
Triceratops.

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:38.960
Tapioca.

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:40.060
Racquetball.

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:41.380
Stuccatto!”

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:43.640
“Me llamo Jaime!”

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:44.280
“Pumpernickel!

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:45.280
Pudding!”

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:47.480
She helps write the commercials

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:53.030
In 2009, Courtney told Cleveland.com about
the backstory she envisioned for Flo, saying,

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:57.640
"When I picture Flo in life she drives her
little Mini Cooper around town and it's basically

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:01.300
like when Bob Hoskins drove into Toontown
in Who Framed Roger Rabbit.

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:04.500
Basically all the buildings and the birds
are singing in her mind."

00:02:04.789 --> 00:02:08.660
Courtney explained to Lohud that over the
years, she's worked with the writers to shape

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:09.480
the arc of her character.

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:14.180
"The writers are very open to what I think;
there may be some improv here now and then,

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:15.530
that is encouraged, by the way.

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:18.920
All of us were working so hard to find out
who this person was and what the boundaries

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:19.440
were.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:23.380
With Flo, she was sort of originally this
perfect little Number One employee, but the

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:24.560
power has gotten to her."

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:29.850
Uh oh, sounds like her little sidekick dude,
Jamie, might want to sleep with one eye open.

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:31.820
“Jamie, don’t forget to clean the fridge
when you’re done.”

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:34.080
“She seems nice.”

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:35.440
“She seems nice…

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:39.160
She’s actually pretty nice.”

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:40.680
She lived in France

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:43.880
When Courtney was just a child, her family
spent a year in France.

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:48.290
Though she later forgot how to speak the language,
the experience came in handy during her college

00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:52.000
years when she spent a semester abroad with
a host family in Avignon.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:56.980
Still, she told The Nerdist's JV Club podcast
that the culture shock of returning to France

00:02:56.980 --> 00:02:57.480
was difficult.

00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:02.240
"I ate, like, olympic amounts of bread and
cheese, and then I outgrew all of my clothes

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:07.160
and I remember, I was so sad, like, I really
looked like Gerard Depardieu.

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:11.720
I couldn't afford a haircut, like, no makeup,
and I'd like mope around France because I

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:12.940
felt so bad about myself."

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:15.000
She's a playwright

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:19.800
Before landing the life-changing role of Flo,
Courtney penned a play called Those Courtney

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:20.300
Girls.

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:24.740
Based on exaggerated versions of herself and
her sister, Jennifer, and their lives as broke

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:28.380
actors, Those Courtney Girls ran at the 2000
Aspen Comedy Festival.

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:33.320
Though Courtney says it didn't make a "big
splash," it did help her make more contacts

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:35.900
in the comedy business — networking that
has clearly paid off.

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:37.620
Way to go, Flo.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:39.100
Thanks for watching!

